## Context  
Seasonality significantly impacts the underlying and immediate factors that contribute to acute malnutrition (AMN). It refers to recurring patterns within a year [@tsR], such as specific months or quarters when AMN cases rise or fall.  

Understanding the seasonal variation of AMN is crucial for evidence-based decision-making. Nonetheless, despite a general awareness that AMN has a seasonal variation, limited evidence exists to substantiate it. Consequently, stakeholders often rely on assumptions of an AMN seasonal peak during the food security lean season [@africadry] and, to account for this, nutrition surveys are routinely timed to coincide with the lean season each year.

The limited understanding of AMN’s seasonal variation has been attributed to two key challenges: (i) the availability of robust analytical methods and (ii) access to primary data [@africadry]. The first challenge can now be mitigated by employing the time series data science. The second challenge, in its turn, can be mitigated by leveraging high-quality secondary data, such as survey datasets or treatment program admissions. Studies using 15 years of SMART survey data from African drylands have underscored the complex relationship between AMN and acute food insecurity (AFI), revealing that they do not always align across seasons or livelihoods [@africadry; @anastasia]. Notably, this research identified two AMN seasonal peaks: April-May and August-September [@africadry].  

In Somalia, the seasonality of AMN is generaly associated with the seasonality of food security as well. There are five defined food security seasons as described by @ENN:  




```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-seasonal-calendar
%%| fig-cap: "Somalia food security seasonal calendar"
%%| fig-cap-location: bottom
gantt
    dateFormat M
    axisFormat %b
    todayMarker off
    section Seasons
    Jilaal :1, 4
    Gu     :4, 7
    Post-Gu:7, 8
    Hagaa  :7, 10
    Deyr   :10, 12
```




  + **Jilaal**: the most extended dry season, characterized by livestock migration and decreased milk production in the pastoralist livelihoods. 
  + **Gu**: characterized by heavy rainfall, it constitutes Somalia's 70% of the crop production season, and livestock production depends upon this season.
  + **Post-Gu**: characterized by increased pasture and water availability for the livestock and increased availability of milk, fruits, and vegetables. This is also the peak season of a liquid butter made of milk known as *ghee*.  
  + **Hagaa**: a dry season, characterized by light rainfalls.  
  + **Deyr**: a rainy season, however localized and less reliable.

It is generally assumed that AMN in Somalia peaks from the end of the Jilaal season to the end of the Gu season. An analysis by @ENN, based on SMART surveys conducted by the Somalia Food Security and Nutrition Analysis Unit (FSNAU) and partners, with data collected between the Gu and the Deyr seasons from 2014 to 2021, identified two distinct seasonal peaks: **May/June (the beginning of Gu)** and **November (mid-Deyr)**, with a decline during the lean season. The study also highlighted variations of these patterns based on livelihoods, including pastoralist, agro-pastoralist, and riverine/farming communities:

  + Among the pastoralist communities, the peak in AMN was observed in June (Gu), which did not align with the food security lean season, as pastoral consumption typically improves starting in April, when animals give birth, coinciding with the onset of rainfall. During this period, households gain access to milk, meat, and income from the sale of animals [@ENN]. Moreover, a second peak was noted in November, a period when food insecurity is generally expected to be minimal following the Gu harvest [@ENN].

  + In the agropastoralist and farming areas, the seasonality also followed the above described peaks [@ENN]. No nadir was reported in this study.

### What does this analysis add?
  This analysis examines six years of severe acute malnutrition (SAM) admission data from treatment programs in Somalia, to explore patterns in admissions, with a focus on **trends and seasonality** over time.

#### Objectives
  + **Identify and describe trend patterns**: a long-term direction or movement in the admissions that persists across the series. This represents underlying patterns in the admissions after removing short-term fluctuations. 

  + **Identify and describe the seasonal patterns**: recurring patterns that occur in a fixed and specific period every year. In this analysis, this refers to monthly recurring patterns over the years.




{{< pagebreak >}}






## Methods
### Data source
The analysis used the [Somalia Nutrition Cluster's](https://response.reliefweb.int/somalia/nutrition) six years of country-wide data on SAM admission into AMN treatment programme. The data is reported monthly at district-level. 

The initial analysis aimed to use data on global acute malnutrition. However, data on moderate acute malnutrition (MAM) were excluded due to unstable variance caused by frequent stockouts of read-to-use supplementary food (RUSF) in the programme. Including MAM data in the analysis could have led to misleading findings.

### Data wrangling {#sec-data-wrangling} 
It consisted in excluding districts with missing admissions from January to December; then by aggregating the sum of the total number of children admitted by month at the national level, and by livelihood systems (Pastoral, Agropastoral, Riverine and Urban/IDPs), as demonstrated in the flowchart below.



```{mermaid}
%%| label: wrangling-workflow
%%| fig-cap: Data wrangling workflow
flowchart LR
    A[(Dataset)]
    B[Exclusion of districts with frequent missing of admissions data]
    C[Summarise admissions by **month** at the national level]
    D[Summarise admissions by **month** by livelihood systems]
    E[Time series]
    F([National])
    G([By livelihood systems])

    A --> B --> C --> E
    B --> D --> E
    E --> F
    E --> G
```




::: {.callout-note}
Twelve districts were excluded for frequent missing of admissions data (see @fig-exclusion-inclusion): Ceel Dheere, Jalalaqsi, Jamaame, Kurtunwaarey, Sablaale, Adan Yabaal, Bu'aale, Jilib, Saakow/Salagle, Sheik, Cadale, and Xarardheere. 
:::




```{r}
#| label: fig-exclusion-inclusion
#| fig-cap: "Districts included and excluded during data wrangling"
#| fig-width: 4
#| fig-height: 5
#| fig-dpi: 200
map_incl_excl
```




### Analysis approach  
The data was first explored visually through graphs to identify patterns over time. Then, it was broken down into its components to examine the overall monthly trend and seasonal variation. The average rate of change (ARC) [^1] of the admissions was calculated to better understand the trend. To account for the different livelihood systems within Somalia, the data was split into four separate time series, each representing a different livelihood system.

The analysis was performed using standard statistical tools, which are available in the project’s repository for reference. 

[^1]: The ARC describes the average or typical rate of change (in the admissions of SAM cases in this case) over a given time interval in the time series. 




{{< pagebreak >}}






## Results  

The results will first be presented and described for data grouped at the national level, and then by livelihood systems.

### At the National level  
#### A glance at admissions over time {#sec-glance-national}
The figure below displays the total number of SAM cases admitted into the treatment programme in Somalia over the past six years, from January 2019 to December 2024. It highlights a shifting trend over time: with a relatively stable period before 2022, followed by a remarkable rise, and then a consistent decline that began in 2023. Furthermore, the figure exhibits the existence of a seasonal pattern (the ups and downs) that also varies over time.




```{r}
#| label: fig-timeplot-national
#| fig-cap: "Evolution of admissions over time"
tsplot_national
```




#### A glance at the seasonal variation  
@fig-seasonal-plot-national displays the evolution of admissions across months (x-axis). Each line represents the evolution of the admissions throughout the months of a particular year. 

From this figure, the seasonal variation starts to be seen, though not clearly yet. Nevertheless, two patterns can be spotted from the figure:  

  1. Before 2022, the seasonal variation seemed to be regular each year, with a relatively shallow amplitude between each lines.  
  2. As of 2022, the amplitude between lines rose, and the seasonality shifted. 




```{r}
#| label: fig-seasonal-plot-national
#| fig-cap: "A glance at the seasonal patterns over the past six years"
ssnplot_national
```




The patterns in the above figure can further be visualized below with the data split into the admissions before 2022, and as of 2022.

