### Decomposed components of admissions over time  
A time series can exhibit a variety of underlying patterns that may not be easliy visible from a regular time plot (an attempt made in above sections, however without a clear visual). By decomposing the time series into its constituent components, these hidden patterns can be brought to light. The following sections reveal the extracted patterns from the admissions data, beginning with the national level analysis, followed by insights at the livelihood system. 

#### At the National level  
In the figure below, the first graph (top-down) is the whole data (the admissions); this is the same graph shown in @fig-timeplot-national. The second graph is the **trend pattern alone** over time. The third graph is the **seasonal pattern alone** over time, after removing the trend. The fourth graph represents any other variation in the admissions that is not caused by neither of the above. This is referred to as remainder or noise.  




```{r}
#| label: fig-cmpnts-national
#| fig-cap: "A glance at the components of the admissions at the national level"
cmpnts_plot_national
```




##### The trend component in focus 

All in all, as highlighted in the @sec-glance-national, the trend pattern exhibits distinct characteristics. In terms of **shape**, it is non-linear. In terms of **stability**, the trend evolves over time, indicating a dynamic rather than a stable pattern. In terms of **direction**, it exhibits a notable rise in admissions starting in the last quarter of 2021, followed by a decline that began to decline in 2023.  

In the figure below, the trend line is split into the times when there was a notable change in the direction of the trend over time. To quantify and understand the magnitude of the change in graphs, the ARC [@arc] was calculated for each slope. Their results are summarised in @tbl-arc-national.




```{r}
#| label: fig-piecewise-slopes
#| fig-cap: "Periods of significant shifts in the direction of the trend"
#| fig-subcap: 
#|   - "Jan-Jun 2019"
#|   - "Jul-Oct 2019"
#|   - "Nov 2019-Feb 2020"
#|   - "Mar 2020-Jan 2021"
#|   - "Feb 2021-Mar 2023"
#|   - "Apr 2023-Dec 2024"
#| layout-ncol: 3
#| layout-nrow: 2

slope_beforeJul2019
slope_jul2019_oct2019
slope_nov2019_feb2020
slope_mar2020_jan2021
slope_feb2021_mar2023
slope_april2023_dec2024
```




::: {#nte-read-arc .callout-note}
## How to read Table 2 in conjunction with Figure 10?

**Example 1: increase**  
In slope (a), the trend starts in January 2019 with `r format(round(arc_beforeJul2019[[1]]),F,big.mark=",")` cases admitted. It increased to `r format(round(arc_beforeJul2019[[3]]),F,big.mark=",")` cases by June of the same year. Between this period of `r arc_beforeJul2019[[5]]` months, the admissions increased by an average of `r round(arc_beforeJul2019[[6]])` cases each month.

**Example 2: decrease**  
In slope (f), the trend starts in April 2023, with `r format(round(arc_april2023_dec2024[[1]]),F, big.mark=",")` cases admitted. It decreased to `r format(round(arc_april2023_dec2024[[3]]),F,big.mark=",")` cases by December 2024. During this period of `r arc_april2023_dec2024[[5]]` months, the admissions decreased by an average of `r format(round(arc_april2023_dec2024[[6]]),big.mark=",")` cases each month. 
:::

| Slope | Time interval | Admissions~i~ | Admissions~f~ | ARC |  
| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |
| (a) | `r arc_beforeJul2019[[5]]` | `r format(round(arc_beforeJul2019[[1]]),F,big.mark=",")` | `r format(round(arc_beforeJul2019[[3]]),F,big.mark=",")` | `r round(arc_beforeJul2019[[6]])` |  
| (b) | `r arc_aug2019_oct2019[[5]]` | `r format(round(arc_aug2019_oct2019[[1]]),F, big.mark=",")` | `r format(round(arc_aug2019_oct2019[[3]]),F,big.mark=",")` | `r round(arc_aug2019_oct2019[[6]])` | 
| (c) | `r arc_nov2019_feb2020[[5]]` | `r format(round(arc_nov2019_feb2020[[1]]),F,big.mark=",")` | `r format(round(arc_nov2019_feb2020[[3]]),F,big.mark=",")` | `r round(arc_nov2019_feb2020[[6]])` | 
| (d) | `r arc_mar2020_jan2021[[5]]` | `r format(round(arc_mar2020_jan2021[[1]]),F,big.mark=",")` | `r format(round(arc_mar2020_jan2021[[3]]),F,big.mark=",")` | `r round(arc_mar2020_jan2021[[6]])` | 
| (e) | `r arc_feb2021_mar2023[[5]]` | `r format(round(arc_feb2021_mar2023[[1]]),F,big.mark=",")` | `r format(round(arc_feb2021_mar2023[[3]]),F, big.mark=",")` | `r format(round(arc_feb2021_mar2023[[6]]),big.mark=",")` | 
| (f) | `r arc_april2023_dec2024[[5]]` | `r format(round(arc_april2023_dec2024[[1]]),F, big.mark=",")` | `r format(round(arc_april2023_dec2024[[3]]),F,big.mark=",")` | `r format(round(arc_april2023_dec2024[[6]]),big.mark=",")` | 

: The average rate of change of admissions by month in the given periods, as represented in Figure 10 {#tbl-arc-national}

<br> 

##### The seasonal component in focus  {#sec-sp-national}  
All in all, the seasonal component of the national level data reveals a consistent seasonal pattern in the admissions, though it is **time-varying**. The **highest peak** of admissions occurs between **May and June**, preceded by a **low nadir consistently observed in April**, which starts in February. The first peak occurs in January, following an increase in admissions starting in November.

While the patterns remain consistent over the years, the amplitude[^2] of both the peaks and nadirs has varied over time. Before 2022, the peak was higher and sharper, while the nadir was relatively shallower. Conversely, as of 2022 onwards, the amplitude of the peak flattened, while the nadir became remarkably sharper.  

[^2]: The amplitude of a peak or nadir refers to the difference between the peak or nadir's value and the average trend the level. The latter is represented by zero on the y-axis of the seasonal plot. 




```{r}
#| label: fig-seasonal-pattern-national
#| fig-cap: "A glance at the seasonality of admissions with the data at the national level"
seasonal_cmpnt_national
```




The seasonal component before and as of 2022 can be visualized in @fig-seasonal-pattern-natioanal-b2022, and in @fig-seasonal-pattern-natioanal-a2022 respectively

::: {.panel-tabset}
