###### The seasonal component in focus   
The seasonal pattern in this livelihood system exhibits a distinct time-varying nature, with several peaks and significant changes in theirs amplitude, as clearly shown in the “before and as of 2022” plots.




```{r}
#| label: fig-seasonal-pattern-riverine
#| fig-cap: "A glance at the seasonality of admissions in the riverine livelihood system"
seasonal_cmpnt_riverine
```




Before 2022, there used to be two peaks as well as two nadirs. Concerning the peaks, the high peak was observed in December, and another peak (lower than December's) between May and June. Concerning the nadirs, similar to that described in the agropastorals, there were two with the same level of depth, but different durations. The long-lasting nadir was observed between August and November, while a second, and short-lasting one, was observed in April (@fig-seasonal-pattern-riverine-b2022). 

As for 2022, there was a significant change in the amplitude patterns. Several peaks now appear throughout the year. Unlike the other livelihood systems, the months of May and June no longer exhibit the high peak. Instead, the high peak is observed in December, followed by three additional peaks of similar amplitude in January, March, and August (@fig-seasonal-pattern-riverine-a2022). Notably, in the Riverine system, and contrast to all other areas, the month of March shows an increase in admissions. As for nadirs, the deepest one is observed in November.

::: {.panel-tabset}
