###  From graphs to calendar: a simple view of AMN seasonality

Currently, the analyses parameters of the AMN go hand-in-hand with those of the IPC AFI, as visually demostrated in @fig-afiamncalendar for Gu- and Deyr-based assessments.

![Current Somalia's AFI and AMN seasonal calendar for the IPC analyses](data/som-ipc-parameters.png){#fig-afiamncalendar}

::: {.callout-note}
The findings of this analysis suggest that the parameters used in AMN analysis should be reconsidered to better align with the actual periods of change in AMN trends. These parameters should be sensitive to the distinct characteristics of existing livelihood systems.
:::

The seasonal calendars of AMN by livelihood system are presented below, based on the seasonal variation graphs shown in the preceding sections of this report, taking the patterns as of 2022 as a reference. These graphs illustrate the frequency and duration of AMN seasons, with peak periods shown in red and nadir periods in blue. Amongst the peaks and nadirs, the highest peak and lowest nadir are highlighted where applicable. 

#### Pastoral livelihood system 

In this livelihood system, the peak periods occur three times, while the nadirs occur two times. Here, given that the amplitude of the peaks is nearly the same, two high peaks are hence highlighted as "high peak 1" and "high peak 2". This is unique to this livelihood system. 

High peak 1 occurs from May towards the end of June, and high peak 2 from October towards the end of January. As for the nadir, there is only one deepest nadir that occurs from February towards the end of April.

::: {.gantt-pastoral}



```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-amn-seasonal-calendar-pastoral
%%| fig-cap: Pastoral livelihood systems' AMN seasonal calendar
gantt
    dateFormat M
    axisFormat %b
    todayMarker off
    section AMN Seasons
    Peak :1, 2
    Low nadir :2, 5
    High peak 1 :5, 7
    Nadir :7, 10
    High peak 2 :10, 12
```



:::

#### Agropastoral livelihood system 

In this livelihood system, both the peak and nadir periods occurs two times each. The highest peak occurs from May towards the end of September. The deepest nadir occurs from February towards the end of April.

::: {.gantt-agropastoral}



```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-amn-seasonal-calendar-agropastoral
%%| fig-cap: Agropastoral livelihood systems' AMN seasonal calendar
gantt
    dateFormat M
    axisFormat %b
    todayMarker off
    section AMN Seasons
    Peak :1, 2
    Low nadir :2, 5
    High peak :5, 9
    Nadir :9, 11
    Peak : 11, 12
```



:::

#### Riverine livelihood system 

In this livelihood system, both the peak and nadir periods occurs three times each. The highest peak occurs from November towards the end of March. The deepest nadir occurs from September towards the end of October.

::: {.gantt-riverine}



```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-amn-seasonal-calendar-riverine
%%| fig-cap: Riverine livelihood systems' AMN seasonal calendar
gantt
    dateFormat M
    axisFormat %b
    todayMarker off
    section AMN Seasons
    High peak :1, 3
    Nadir :3, 4
    Peak: 4, 5
    Nadir :5, 7
    Peak :7, 9
    Low nadir :9, 11
    High peak :11, 12
```



:::

#### Urban/IDPs livelihood system 

In this livelihood system, both the peak and nadir periods occurs two times each. The highest peak occurs from May towards the end of August. The deepest nadir occurs from January towards the end of April.

::: {.gantt-urban-idps}



```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-amn-seasonal-calendar-urbanipds
%%| fig-cap: Urban/IDPs livelihood systems' AMN seasonal calendar
gantt
    dateFormat M
    axisFormat %b
    todayMarker off
    section AMN Seasons
    Low nadir :1, 5
    High peak :5, 8
    Nadir :8, 9
    Peak :9, 12
```



:::

### Do AMN seasonal patterns align with food security seasonality?

All in all, the unveiled seasonal patterns of AMN do not fully align with the seasonality of AFI, as it was theoretically believed to be. This is true for the Jilaal season in particular, where most of its period falls under the AMN low season and the very small portion of the AMN high season is a spill-over from the Deyr season that precedes it. It is also true for the Gu season, during which food availability starts to increase, compared to the Jilaal season, nevertheless, the AMN observes its highest peak in all livelihood systems, except in the Reverines. As for the Deyr season, it tends to align with the AMN's second high season in all livelihood systems. This relationship can be visualized in @fig-afi-amn-calendar-pastoral, @fig-afi-amn-calendar-agropastoral, @fig-afi-amn-calendar-riverine and @fig-afi-amn-calendar-urbanidps

