## Discussion  
Tackling acute malnutrition necessitates comprehensive and up-to-date information on its evolution over time (the times of the year when cases rise or fall), space, and its corresponding magnitude. This analysis aimed to provide evidence-based information on the evolution of the trend of SAM cases and their corresponding seasonal variation over the past six years. It was based on using SAM admissions data in their original form.  It is worth remembering that admissions are fundamentally a function of case-finding capacity and programme effectiveness. Consequently, the results of the analysis are also a reflection of that. This means that if these factors are sub-optimal, the observed patterns may represent false patterns or artefacts of incomplete data. Nevertheless, the data used in this analysis had an optimal reporting rate (≥80%) across catchment areas over months and years. This strengthens the reliability of the data and the confidence in the patterns gleaned, suggesting that they likely reflect the genuine trends and seasonal variation in SAM admissions rather than being the result of incomplete data.

All in all, the analysis reveals that the **magnitude of children admitted into treatment programme has increased remarkably** as of the third quarter of 2021, until 2023 (`r arc_feb2021_mar2023[[5]]` months), when it started to exhibit a downward trend (@tbl-arc-national). The average number of cases admitted during this period increased by `r format(round(arc_feb2021_mar2023[[6]]),big.mark=",")` cases each month. The end of 2021 was the time when the country had been hit by a severe drought that had aggravated even more the already existing and persistent vulnerabilities. As a result, several livelihoods were on the brink of Famine [@FRC]. The increased trend reflects the intensified programme response at the time, to fit the also increased needs. Moreover, the results show, as well, that even after the drought has passed, the magnitude of the admissions has remained excessively high, compared to years before the drought, although it shows a downward trend. This suggests that the programme response across the country has remained high even after. Looking ahead, the shallow decline observed in the slope suggests that the magnitude of admissions could remain high in the following years before catching up with the pattern before the drought. This means that in case the programme becomes underfunded in the short-term, the trend line might portray a false improvement in the situation (by lowering the magnitude), as this would be the result of many children who have been left untreated due to the scale-down of programme response capacity.    

As for the **seasonality**, all in all, June is the month when the highest peak of admissions is recorded, with the increase beginning in May. These patterns tend to be consistent with those identified by @ENN using 8-year SMART surveys conducted by the Somalia Food Security and Nutrition Analysis Unit (FSNAU) in rural areas, however, with some nuances. @ENN identified two peaks in the prevalence of acute malnutrition: May, June, and November. One peculiar difference is that in this study, the higher peak was recorded in November, where the average monthly wasting was estimated above 17.5%; and the months of May and June corresponded to the second high peak. Findings from this analysis (based on SAM admissions), contrast considerably in the opposite way: May and June represent the highest peak, which has been consistently occurring over the years, even as of 2022. Concerning the second peak, whilst @ENN identified a second peak in November, this analysis's second high peak is January, however, with an increase beginning in November.

On to the nadir, this analysis's deepest nadir is in April every year. @ENN identified a lower nadir in August and a second in December. From these analysis findings, August used to be the second lowest nadir before 2022, with the data aggregated at the national level. However, as of 2022, this pattern has shifted to September, although the extent of the shift is minimal.  

These analysis findings have shown that the seasonal patterns exhibit notable variations across livelihood systems. In the *pastoral and agropastoral* systems, the highest peak is consistently observed in May and June, and another peak in January, with their beggining in November (pastoral), and December (agropastoral). The lowest nadir occurs in April in both systems. These findings align with @ENN, except for a slight deviation in the agropastoral system, where the second peak shifts from November (as identified by @ENN) to December in this analysis.  

In contrast, the patterns in the riverine system differ significantly from those in other livelihood systems. **It is the only system where the highest peak does not occur in May and June**. These patterns also diverge from @ENN observations, which identified the higher peak in November. In this analysis, November consistently represents the lowest nadir. The highest peak occurs in December to January, followed by several low peaks throughout the year, indicating a more fragmented seasonal pattern.  

::: {.callout-note}
An interesting finding is that the riverine and pastoral are the only livelihood systems where there is a peak in March before the lower nadir in April.
**Why is that??** *Question to the Nutrition Cluster members.*
:::  

As for the *Urban/IDPs livelihood system*, this system exhibits a unique and intriguing pattern compared to the others. While the highest peak consistently occurs in May and June, similar to most other systems (except the riverine), the admissions in January notably align with the trend line (baseline) every year. From February onward, the admissions begin a continuous decline, until they reach the common lowest nadir in April. This distinct pattern could not be compared to previous studies, as they did not include a specific livelihood category for urban areas and/or IDPs.  
**Why is that??** *Question to the Nutrition Cluster members.*   

::: {.callout-important}
The discrepancies in the months in @ENN 's findings may be related to the fact that the survey data used were collected over a specific period, while the admissions are reported monthly over the years. This also explains the absence of a nadir in April in the author's study, as all surveys were undertaken starting in May and then November to December.
:::

Regarding whether the observed AMN seasons follow the AFI's, the results have shown that there is a desialignment, except for the Deyr season. In the Jilaal and the Gu AFI seasons, AMN presents itself in the opposite direction of the AFI, raising questions on the other non-food security factors that play a remarkable role, either protecting children from getting malnourished amidst the lean season that characterizes the Jilaal, or increasing the likelihood of a child getting malnourished amidst an increased food availability that characterizes the Gu season.

According to @ENN,

  > _The pastoral consumption year starts with the onset of the Gu rainy season (April) when species give birth, and households have access to their own milk a meat and income for other essentials from saleable animals_.

This highlights the need for further analysis, including an understanding of the seasonality of childhood diseases in Somalia. 


## Limitations  
As noted, MAM data were excluded from the analysis due to its variance being influenced by program stockouts, resulting in the study not encompassing GAM data. This raises questions about whether similar patterns would emerge if GAM had been analysed. Additionally, the SAM reporting system does not differentiate admissions originating from IDPs versus those from other livelihood systems. This poses a significant limitation in understanding the specific patterns among IDPs. Furthermore, the results reveal that SAM admissions follow a distinct seasonality compared to acute food insecurity, with declines in admissions observed during the lean season. Understanding the factors potentially driving this decline would be critical, but this analysis could not explore these aspects further due to data limitations.

